@@ -307,6 +307,8 @@ OVERRIDE for this user: send work to haiku whenever the task says what to do and
 
 Set it with `omp config set task.routingPreferences "<text>"` or in the settings UI under Tasks.
 
+Subagents that run together cost more than one: five agents on a `$$$` role cost five times one. When two or more subagents run at the same time, each takes the cheapest configured role that has a known price, and the judge is not asked. The group is the largest of: the items in the task call, one plus this parent's subagents already running, and this parent's spawns that have started and not finished. The three are never added. Separate calls that start together, such as parallel tool calls or a `Promise.all` of eval `agent()` calls, see each other through the last count. A streamed call is only complete when streaming ends, so with two or more roles an early launch waits for that before it picks a role. The routing note says `(cheapest role: 5 subagents start together)`. A role with no known price is never taken as the cheap one, and with no priced role the judge decides. A hard job in a group gets the cheapest role too: run it alone, or name its model with `model`, when it needs a stronger one.
+
 Automatic selection applies to every spawn that carries no model of any kind (no per-call `model`, no host `model`), whether it comes from the task tool or from an eval `agent()`/`workpool()` call. It takes precedence over `task.agentModelOverrides` and agent frontmatter. With exactly one usable role alias there is nothing to decide, so that alias is chosen without calling the judge and the routing note says `only one configured alias`. With two or more, the judgment has a four-second timeout; classification always runs, and there is no fallback role. If native judgment is unavailable, fails, times out or is insufficiently confident, no model is chosen and the reason is reported. With no usable alias either, a `task.agentModelOverrides` entry or the agent frontmatter `model` is used (it is your own configuration) with a visible warning that names the reason, and without either the spawn fails before the child starts with that reason. The parent's model is never used. Caller cancellation propagates instead of being reported as a failed classification. The result and progress routing note identifies the choice and its reason.
 
 The native judge needs the `judge` role to resolve first (after its own configured fallback chain and the credentialed models) to a model whose API is a native judgment API: TypeSafe (`typesafe/jev-latest` with `TYPESAFE_API_KEY`), OpenRouter Decisions, Workers AI or OpenAI Decisions. A judge role that resolves to an ordinary chat model is not a native judge.
diff --git a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
index 6181a0ab8b..16d999a9c4 100644
--- a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 - Running background tasks now have their own pinned shelf directly above the Subagents shelf, in the same style: backgrounded `bash` commands, backgrounded `eval` cells and services, each as a row with its id (such as `bg_1099`), a one-line preview of the command and its elapsed time. Jobs that run a subagent stay in the Subagents shelf. `display.pinnedJobs` (`collapsed` by default, `full`, `off`) sets how many rows show before an expander, as `display.pinnedAgents` does for subagents.
 - Name an operator-configured model role such as `@worker` in a task item's `model`, in a mixed-model batch if you like; it resolves to that role's current model and goes through the same `task.perCallModel` gate and approval as an exact `provider/model-id`.
 - Task subagents called without `model` now get a configured model role chosen by the native judge. When the judge fails, times out, is unsure or is unavailable, no model is chosen and the reason is reported; there is no fallback role. Task results show the requested and resolved model and a `modelRoute` note such as `Automatic model selection: @sonnet.`
+- When two or more subagents run at the same time without `model`, whether from one task call, separate calls that start together, or subagents already running, every one of them now runs the cheapest configured role that has a known price, and the judge is not asked, because the cost of a group is the price of one agent times its size. The routing note says why, for example `(cheapest role: 5 subagents start together)`. A role with no known price is never taken as cheap. With two or more roles, an early launch of a streamed call now waits until the call has finished streaming so the group size is known.
 - Added the `task.routingPreferences` setting (empty by default): free-text guidance, in your own words, for which kinds of work the automatic subagent model choice should give to the cheaper model. The judge follows it ahead of its general leanings but never picks a role too weak for hard or risky work. The default routing is unchanged; empty or blank sends the judge exactly what it got before.
 - Answer MCP form elicitation requests (`elicitation/create`) with the same dialog the `ask` tool uses. An approval with no fields, such as Executor's "may I run this action?", is one Accept/Decline question; a form is one question per field plus a final `Submit this form?`. Dismissing the dialog, choosing to chat instead, a timed-out answer, an unsupported host (no ask dialog) or any error cancels the request and never accepts it. URL mode is refused.
 - The task tool description now lists your model roles as `@role` and tells agents to pass the role when you name a model or tier such as "haiku", instead of guessing a model version. When the agent cannot tell which model you mean, it asks with the `ask` tool.
